Version 3.0 of the[Nakama Unreal SDK](https://github.com/heroiclabs/nakama-unreal) is out today. It’s a big update with three aims: make the API consistent, move to a modern async pattern, and make Nakama easier to use from C++.


## A more consistent API


v3 brings the Unreal SDK in line with the rest of our client libraries, and makes it consistent with itself:


- **Aligned naming:** Function names now match across our SDKs, so what you learn in Unreal carries over to our other clients and docs.
- **Two headers:** Types that were spread across lots of small header files now live in` NakamaTypes.gen.h` and` NakamaRtTypes.gen.h` .
- **Consistent responses:** Every call reports success or failure the same way.


## Modern, future-based async


In v2, every call took two delegates, one for success and one for failure. Even a simple device login meant declaring both delegates, binding both handlers, and writing two separate callback functions:


```text
FOnAuthUpdate    AuthenticationSuccessDelegate  ;
AuthenticationSuccessDelegate  .  AddDynamic  (  this  ,    &  ASagiShiActor  ::  OnAuthenticationSuccess  );


FOnError    AuthenticationErrorDelegate  ;
AuthenticationErrorDelegate  .  AddDynamic  (  this  ,    &  ASagiShiActor  ::  OnAuthenticationError  );


Client  ->  AuthenticateDevice  (  DeviceId  ,    Username  ,    bCreate  ,    Vars  ,    AuthenticationSuccessDelegate  ,    AuthenticationErrorDelegate  );


void    ASagiShiActor  ::  OnAuthenticationSuccess  (  UNakamaSession  *    LoginData  )
{
UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Log  ,    TEXT  (  "Login successful: %s"  ),    *  LoginData  ->  SessionData  .  Username  );
}


void    ASagiShiActor  ::  OnAuthenticationError  (  const    FNakamaError  &    Error  )
{
UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Error  ,    TEXT  (  "Failed to login: %s"  ),    *  Error  .  Message  );
}


```


In v3, calls return a future, and you chain a single` .Next()` callback that receives a result object. Check` bIsError` , then use the value or the error:


```text
Nakama  ::  AuthenticateDevice  (  ClientConfig  ,    bCreate  ,    Username  ,    DeviceId  ).  Next  (
[  WeakThis  ](  const    FNakamaSessionResult  &    AuthenticateResult  )
{
if    (  AuthenticateResult  .  bIsError  )
{
UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Error  ,    TEXT  (  "Failed to log in: %s"  ),    *  AuthenticateResult  .  Error  .  Message  );
return  ;
}


if    (  !  WeakThis  .  IsValid  ())
{
return  ;
}


WeakThis  ->  Session    =    AuthenticateResult  .  Value  ;
UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Log  ,    TEXT  (  "Log in successful: %s"  ),    *  AuthenticateResult  .  Value  .  Username  );
});


```


Success and error handling now sit right next to the call, so the code reads top to bottom instead of being spread across bound delegates and member functions.


**One thing to watch:** the callback runs after the calling function has returned, so don’t capture locals by reference. Keep your config and session as members of a long-lived object, like your actor, and capture a weak pointer to it, as in the example above.


## A more convenient API


- **Plain structs instead of client objects:**` UNakamaClient` and` UNakamaSession` used to be` UObject` s. They’re now the` USTRUCT` s` FNakamaClientConfig` and` FNakamaSession` , so there’s no client object to create or keep alive. Build a config once and pass it to the calls you make.
- **Namespaced calls:** Regular API calls live under` Nakama::` and realtime calls under` NakamaRt::` , with` FNakamaRtConnection` handling the realtime socket.
- **Simpler realtime connection:** You no longer need a pair of delegates and handler functions to connect:


```text
RtConnection    =    MakeShared  <  FNakamaRtConnection  >  ();


const    FNakamaWebSocketConnectionParams    ConnectionParams    =    {    Host  ,    Session  .  Token  ,    PingIntervalSeconds  ,    Port  ,    bUseSSL    };


RtConnection  ->  Connect  (  ConnectionParams  ).  Next  ([](  const    FNakamaWebSocketConnectionResult  &    ConnectionResult  )
{
if    (  ConnectionResult  .  ErrorCode    !=    ENakamaWebSocketError  ::  None  )
{
UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Error  ,    TEXT  (  "Socket connection failed: %d"  ),    (  uint8  )  ConnectionResult  .  ErrorCode  );
return  ;
}


UE_LOG  (  LogTemp  ,    Log  ,    TEXT  (  "Socket connection succeeded"  ));
});


```


## Upgrading from v2


Upgrading takes a handful of structural changes: replace the plugin, update` Build.cs` , switch to the new call pattern, and update your includes and realtime type names. If your project uses the old async Blueprint nodes, we’ve included a script that removes them safely before you upgrade, because Unreal doesn’t cope well with broken async nodes.


To help developers who are planning on migrating their existing projects from v2 to v3, we put together a handy[migration guide](https://heroiclabs.com/docs/nakama/client-libraries/unreal/migration-guide/) to help you along the way.


